# Lesson 4, batch 2: is a finesse the right play? Dummy's ♦A is its only outside entry; use it for the clubs (duck one round first), not for a spade finesse.
title: Save the entry for the clubs
id: L04-28
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.7, 3.5
student: S
board: 110
dealer: E
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  862    76     A5     AK6542
  E  T943   532    Q73    QJ9
  S  AQ5    AK4    KJ862  73
  W  KJ7    QJT98  T94    T8

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 7
  spades: 1 = The ace. The queen too, if East has the king and you lead toward it from dummy.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 2 = Your king and dummy's ace.
  clubs: 2 = The ace and king. Three more if they split 3-2 and you can reach dummy after setting them up.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Dummy has only one way in outside clubs. What is it worth most for?
play:
  1   W HQ  N H6  E H2  S H4*
      note: Seven obvious winners. The clubs can give five tricks if they split 3-2, but you'll have to lose one club first, and you'll need dummy's ♦A to get back. A spade finesse would use up that entry, so leave the spades alone.
      H4 key: Duck. You'll lose the lead once in clubs anyway. Duck a heart now, and East may have no heart left when East gets in.
      H4 not *: Did you think about ducking? You'll have to give up a club trick, and whoever wins it will lead a heart. Lose a heart now, while you still have the ace and king.
  2   W HJ  N H7  E H3  S HA
      note: Now win the ace. Your king still stops the hearts.
  3   S C3  W C8  N C2*  E C9
      note: Now the clubs.
      C2 key: Duck. You have to lose a club anyway, so lose it now, while dummy still has the ace and king to get back in.
      C2 not *: Did you think about ducking? With the ace and king gone, you'd have to give up a club and then need another way back to dummy. Lose the first round, and dummy's ace and king bring the rest home.
  4   E H5  S HK  W H8  N S2
      note: East had one heart left. Your king wins.
  5   S C7  W CT  N CA  E CJ
      note: Over to dummy's clubs. Both opponents followed twice.
  6   N CK  E CQ  S D2  W D4
      note: The clubs split 3-2. All of dummy's clubs are winners.
  7   N C6  E S3  S D6  W S7
  8   N C5  E S4  S D8  W H9
  9   N C4  E D3  S S5  W D9
  10  N DA  E D7  S DJ  W DT
      note: That's nine tricks.
  11  N D5  E DQ  S DK  W HT
  12  S SA  W SJ  N S6  E S9
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  13  S SQ  W SK  N S8  E ST
      note: 10 tricks: 3NT made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: seven obvious winners, and five club tricks if they split 3-2.
  Dummy's ♦A was its only way in, and the clubs needed it more than a
  spade finesse did.

  You ducked the first club while dummy still had its ace and king, so the
  rest of the clubs ran without needing any other entry. Here the spade
  finesse would have lost too, but that wasn't the point: one trick from a
  finesse is worth less than five from a long suit. A finesse is one tool,
  not the only one.
